WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:05.060
Apple has finally updated their MacBook Pros
for 2019, and quite a few people seem to be

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:09.820
confused on which configuration they should
buy, so we decided to make this video highlighting

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:12.639
10 mistakes you should avoid when making your
purchase.

00:00:12.639 --> 00:00:17.380
And stick around till the end to see which
configurations we recommend for every use-case.

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:22.160
The first 5 mistakes have to do with the new
15” MacBook Pro, so let’s get into mistake

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:23.210
#1.

00:00:23.210 --> 00:00:29.760
When buying the 8-core MacBook Pro, don’t
spend the extra $200 on the 2.4GHz CPU upgrade,

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:36.800
because the 2.3GHz processor is already an
i9, and in the real-world Cinebench R20 benchmark,

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:40.079
we got a score of 3,161 points.

00:00:40.079 --> 00:00:45.949
Snazzy Labs tested the 2.4GHz 8-core processor
and he scored 3,231 points.

00:00:45.949 --> 00:00:51.980
That’s only a 2% higher score for $200,
so we don’t recommend anyone to make that

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:52.980
upgrade.

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:57.079
Onto mistake #2, if you’re planning on getting
the 8-core processor, start by selecting the

00:00:57.079 --> 00:01:03.489
$2800 model that already comes with an 8-core
instead of choosing the $2400 model and upgrading

00:01:03.489 --> 00:01:05.040
the CPU to an 8 core.

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:11.550
The reason is that it makes you upgrade straight
to the 2.4GHz CPU for $300, and at that point,

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:17.430
you’re already at $2700 and you only get
a 256GB SSD.

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:23.650
Upgrading to 512 brings the price up to $2900,
so just choose the $2800 model from the very

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:29.010
beginning which comes with the 512GB SSD and
of course, the 8-core CPU.

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:33.200
For the Third mistake, don’t upgrade the
graphics if you’re a photo editor or you’re

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:35.950
doing work that’s heavily dependant on the
processor.

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:41.320
We’ve tested it many times before and upgrading
graphics literally does nothing for photo

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:44.630
editing, so just keep whatever graphics comes
with your configuration.

00:01:44.630 --> 00:01:48.850
The 4th mistake that people make is not upgrading
the RAM and choosing to throw their extra

00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:53.620
cash on something else like the processor
or graphics instead, especially for photo

00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:54.860
editing or similar workloads.

00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:58.260
Yes, it’s a $400 upgrade but it’s well
worth it.

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:02.860
We tested the 2018 MacBook Pros and found
that the biggest factor for photo editing

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:04.960
was actually the RAM, making a bigger difference
than even the faster i9 processor, so prioritize

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:06.640
RAM if you’ve got extra cash.

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:11.770
Now the 5th mistake is made by video editors
or graphics renderers who choose not to buy

00:02:11.770 --> 00:02:13.380
the Vega 20 graphics.

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:18.250
We’ve seen massive boosts in everything
from video editing to gaming performance,

00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:24.050
and even a small jump in processor performance
just going from Radeon 560X graphics to Vega

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:25.050
20.

00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:29.300
If you’re a video editor with extra cash,
pay for Vega 20 before any other upgrade.

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:34.600
The only downside is that Apple makes you
buy the $2800 8-core model in order to upgrade

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:36.920
to Vega 20, but it’s definitely worth it.

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:42.220
Apple has also updated the 13” MacBook Pro
for 2019, so let’s take a look at 5 mistakes

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:44.010
people make when buying that model.

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:49.580
For Mistake #1, people don’t realize that
the $1299 and $1499 models haven’t been

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:55.750
updated at all since 2017, meaning they still
pack outdated 7th-gen dual-core processors

00:02:55.750 --> 00:02:59.830
and the 2nd-gen butterfly keyboard that’s
been having so many issues recently.

00:02:59.830 --> 00:03:04.700
It’s sad that Apple hasn’t updated it,
so we recommend going for the 2018 MacBook

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:09.300
Air instead if you can’t afford the $1800
2019 model.

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:14.630
Mistake #2 is that some people think the new
13” MacBook Pro packs Intel’s latest 9th

00:03:14.630 --> 00:03:16.850
gen processors, but it doesn’t.

00:03:16.850 --> 00:03:19.580
It still packs the same 8th gen processors
from before.

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:21.530
They’re just clocked slightly higher.

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:22.910
Why did Apple do this?

00:03:22.910 --> 00:03:27.819
Well, it turns out that the 9th-gen quad-core
processors don’t come with the faster Intel

00:03:27.819 --> 00:03:32.340
Iris Plus graphics chips which are badly needed
on this machine, so Apple just stuck with

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:33.340
8th gen.

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:35.610
If you want 9th gen, go for the 15”.

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:42.110
Now onto mistake #3, don’t pay the extra
$300 to go from the base i5 to i7 processor.

00:03:42.110 --> 00:03:46.270
It really won’t make much of a difference,
at least not $300 worth.

00:03:46.270 --> 00:03:51.760
A couple of years back, i7 meant that you
get hyper-threading, but these days i5’s

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:56.099
get it as well, so the only difference is
a clock speed boost, so just stick to the

00:03:56.099 --> 00:03:57.810
base CPU on the 13”.

00:03:57.810 --> 00:04:03.260
To avoid mistake #4, make sure to use any
extra cash you have on upgrading to 16GB of

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:04.970
RAM over anything else.

00:04:04.970 --> 00:04:11.190
8GB is really low in 2019 and it’ll benefit
you in everything from web browsing to photo

00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:16.459
and video editing, and basically everything
else, so if you’re gonna upgrade one thing,

00:04:16.459 --> 00:04:17.459
upgrade the RAM.

00:04:17.459 --> 00:04:21.570
However, be careful not to spec out the 13”
MacBook Pro with a bunch of upgrades, which

00:04:21.570 --> 00:04:23.850
brings us to our final mistake.

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:30.000
Some people spec out the 13” with 16GB of
RAM and the faster processor for $2300, not

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:36.230
realizing that the $2400 15” MacBook Pro
gives you a MASSIVE improvement for only $100

00:04:36.230 --> 00:04:37.230
more.

00:04:37.230 --> 00:04:43.960
Yes, you get the larger 15” screen, but
you also get 16GB of faster 2400Mhz DDR4 RAM,

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:50.070
a much faster 9th gen 6-core processor instead
of the 8th gen 4-core, and you actually get

00:04:50.070 --> 00:04:55.690
a dedicated graphics chip which destroys the
13-inch MacBook Pros integrated graphics.

00:04:55.690 --> 00:04:58.340
This’ll make a huge difference for video
editors.

00:04:58.340 --> 00:05:02.350
So with all of that out of the way, let me
give some recommendations for each use-case.

00:05:02.350 --> 00:05:06.960
If you’re a user who does simple stuff like
web browsing, school or business work or using

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:13.300
web-based apps, we recommend just going for
the base $1200 2018 MacBook Air since you

00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:16.440
won’t really be using any of the extra power
the MacBook Pro provides.

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:21.220
Now if you do a little bit of photo or video
editing, maybe some rendering, programming

00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:25.700
or producing any kind of content, you’ll
definitely see the benefits of the MacBook

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:30.540
Pro with its very color accurate display,
extra ports and higher performance, and you

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:36.610
should therefore opt for the $1800 2019 MacBook
Pro, and if you have a bit more cash left

00:05:36.610 --> 00:05:39.550
in your budget, upgrade to 16GB of RAM.

00:05:39.550 --> 00:05:43.760
Now if you’re doing those tasks almost every
day and you take it very seriously, you should

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:50.130
buy the base $2400 15” MacBook Pro, especially
video editors, since you get a dedicated graphics

00:05:50.130 --> 00:05:51.130
chip.

00:05:51.130 --> 00:05:55.460
Photo editors should opt for 32GB of RAM if
they can since that’ll make the biggest

00:05:55.460 --> 00:05:59.900
difference, and that’s the highest config
we recommend, you won’t really get that

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:02.840
much of a boost going for the 8-core.

00:06:02.840 --> 00:06:08.280
Video editors should upgrade to 560X graphics,
and if you really want to upgrade storage

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:14.150
too, just go back and choose the $2800 model
instead because it brings you up to an 8-core

00:06:14.150 --> 00:06:19.430
CPU, and already comes with 560X graphics
and 512GB of storage.

00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:23.729
And if you really want to go all out and have
the fastest machine possible, go for the Vega

00:06:23.729 --> 00:06:28.470
20 upgrade since it makes a huge difference
in video editing or graphically intensive

00:06:28.470 --> 00:06:29.470
tasks.

00:06:29.470 --> 00:06:33.080
So there you have it guys, we’ll have links
to the different configurations in the description

00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:37.419
below, and if this video helped you out, tap
that like button and click the circle above

00:06:37.419 --> 00:06:39.690
to subscribe so you don’t miss out on our
future videos.

00:06:39.690 --> 00:06:45.349
Thanks for watching, and we’ll see you in
the next one!

